note top of SessionManager
**SABER Domain Server & Session Manager**
- Hosts one security domain (scalable to multiple instances)
- Manages multiple concurrent client sessions with timeout and cleanup
- Single point of access for all client interactions via REST and MCP APIs
- Orchestrates task execution and command processing through ExecutionManager
- Handles server lifecycle (start/shutdown) with graceful cleanup
- Coordinates all server components with proper initialization order
- Built for horizontal scaling with load balancing capabilities
- Session timeout management with automatic cleanup of inactive sessions
- Dual protocol handlers: REST API for session/episode management, MCP API for tool execution
end note

note left of SessionMCPAPI
**MCP Protocol Handler**
- Hosts Model Context Protocol server for agent tool access using FastMCP
- Dynamically discovers tools from ExecutionManager via MCPToolGenerator
- Maps MCP tool calls to SessionManager command execution using headers
- Session context obtained from HTTP headers (X-SABER-Session-ID)
- ONLY handles tool discovery and execution via MCP protocol
- Complements REST API which handles all other operations
- Uses header-based session mapping instead of maintaining active session state
end note

note right of SessionRestAPI
**REST Protocol Handler**
- FastAPI-based REST endpoints for session and episode management
- Handles session lifecycle, episode start, policy access, benchmark info
- OpenAPI documentation generation with domain-specific titles
- Includes debug endpoints for cleanup history and session statistics
- NO benchmark orchestration endpoints - that's client responsibility
- Delegates all business logic to SessionManager
end note

note top of BenchmarkManager
**Task Definition Manager (Client Orchestration Model)**
- Focuses purely on task definition management and YAML parsing
- NO server-side benchmark orchestration - that's client responsibility
- Provides task lookup by ID and task configuration access
- BenchmarkSession is minimal wrapper around BenchmarkInfo for API responses
- Task objects contain execution_config, episode_config, and environment specifications
- Environment can be string reference, dict config, or hybrid approach
- BenchmarkConfigLoader handles YAML parsing and validation
- Supports episode configuration extraction for EpisodeManager setup
end note

note top of EpisodeManager
**RL-Style Episode Manager**
- Manages gym-compatible episode lifecycle with automatic termination
- Returns StepResult objects from step() method containing termination signals
- Tracks episode state transitions and step-by-step action history
- Configures episode settings per task from task.episode_config
- Supports max_steps termination and custom termination conditions
- Session-scoped episode tracking with cleanup on session termination
- Validates episode configuration and provides default fallbacks
end note

note top of ExecutionManager
**Enhanced Docker Sandbox Execution Manager with Permanent Environment Support**
Now manages both ephemeral and permanent container environments:
- SandboxEnvironmentManager: Handles session-scoped, ephemeral containers
- PermanentEnvironmentManager: Handles server-scoped, persistent containers
- configure_for_task() method handles Task objects with both sandbox_environment and permanent_environment
- Automatic network bridging between ephemeral and permanent environments
- EnvironmentLoader resolves flexible environment specifications for both types
- Enhanced security validation and multi-network executor management
- Permanent services start with server boot and persist across all episodes
See command_execution_architecture.puml for complete detailed design.
end note

note bottom of EvaluationManager
**Stub Implementation**
- Minimal stub implementation for development support
- Currently only provides logging methods for session and episode events
- Full trajectory tracking and evaluation features to be implemented later
- ActionTracker and TrajectoryStore shown for future implementation reference
end note

